# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Environments
.env
.venv/
venv/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Data: keep the folder structure, ignore everything in it.
# Order matters -- the directory re-include must come before the .gitkeep
# re-include, or git cannot descend far enough to see the file.
data/**
!data/**/
!data/**/.gitkeep

# EVIDENCE, not data. The rule above is right for 56 GB of raw scenes and
# wrong for the handful of small files the published conclusion rests on.
# Until 2026-09-25 none of these were tracked -- including boundary-bands.json,
# which IS the result. 974 KB total, and each one is here for its own reason:
#
#   labels.jsonl              judgement, not computation. Cannot be recomputed
#                             by anyone, ever. Append-only, last write per chip
#                             wins, so it also carries who decided what when.
#   label-sample.json         the draw. Reproducible from its seed only while
#                             candidates-scored.geojson is byte-identical.
#   boundary-bands.json       the published numbers. Regenerating them is a
#                             multi-hour run of 2,000 scattered + 400 shift
#                             trials.
#   candidates-scored.geojson the detection set everything else indexes into.
#                             Regenerable only from the 56 GB of raw scenes.
#
#   review-session.json       the PRE-REGISTRATION for a blind second read:
#                             which chips, drawn from which seed, against a
#                             SHA-256 of labels.jsonl at draw time, with the
#                             protocol and the analysis plan written down
#                             BEFORE the first chip was looked at. Committing
#                             it before the reading starts is the whole of its
#                             evidentiary value -- an analysis plan produced
#                             after the numbers is not an analysis plan.
#   labels-review-*.jsonl     the second reader's verdicts, kept separate from
#                             labels.jsonl so the first read cannot be edited
#                             into agreement with the second.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: candidate-pvessel.json and data/events/corrected/
# (3.2 MB). Both rebuild from the four above in seconds -- WITH ONE
# EXCEPTION, added 2026-10-02. corrected/boundary-bands-point.json does NOT
# rebuild in seconds: it is the same 2,000 scattered + 400 shift trials as
# boundary-bands.json, on the clutter-corrected set, and it is the answer
# this project owed from 26 September to 2 October. The reason given four
# lines above for tracking boundary-bands.json applies to it verbatim, so
# the comment here described something other than what the rule did.
!data/events/labels.jsonl
!data/events/label-sample.json
!data/events/boundary-bands.json
!data/events/candidates-scored.geojson
!data/events/review-session.json
# ...and the archived rounds. The rule above named one exact filename while
# the scheme it serves produces numbered archives, so `git add` refused
# review-session-01.json -- the round whose whole evidentiary value is being
# in the repository.
!data/events/review-session-*.json
!data/events/labels-review-*.jsonl

#   air-bands.json            the aviation result. Regenerates in seconds --
#                             but ONLY from data/raw/aviation-adsbfi/, which
#                             is 109 MB, untracked, and still growing. A clone
#                             cannot rebuild it, which is the same reason
#                             boundary-bands.json is here. It is also a result
#                             AT A WINDOW: re-running tomorrow answers a
#                             different question, because there is more data.
!data/events/air-bands.json

# The airspace geometry the aviation result is about.
#
# data/reference is otherwise ignored because it is fetchable, and NOAA's
# maritime limits are a stable versioned product. FAA NASR is NOT: it is
# reissued on a 28-day cycle. This file is a snapshot carrying its own
# EFF_DATE, and a re-fetch next month moves the 30 nm veil union silently
# underneath a published result -- the same reason boundary-bands.json is
# tracked. The pre-registration says the coordinates are recorded in the
# artefact; recorded means committed.
# Re-include the path down to it, re-exclude each level's contents, then name
# the one file -- otherwise this admits the 3 MB SLA geojson and the NOAA
# shapefile too. Same three-step pattern as corrected/ above.
!data/reference/
data/reference/*
!data/reference/airspace/
data/reference/airspace/*
!data/reference/airspace/appendix-d-airports.json

# The corrected nulls. See the EXCEPTION note above: a multi-hour run, not a
# seconds-long rebuild.
# Three lines, in this order, and the order is the whole of it: git will not
# descend into an excluded directory, so the directory is re-included first;
# that alone would admit all ten files in it, so everything inside is
# re-excluded; and then the one file is named.
!data/events/corrected/
data/events/corrected/*
!data/events/corrected/boundary-bands-point.json

# And the per-scene record, for the same reason one step further out: without
# it nothing can be VERIFIED. `scripts/reproduce.py` re-runs the pipeline and
# compares against the committed numbers, and the only stage with a committed
# reference to check against is `boundary_analysis.py` -- which needs the
# searched grids in sar-* and the matched/unmatched split in dark-*. A clone
# without these can execute four stages and confirm nothing.
#
# 21.3 MB on disk, 1.8 MB in git: geojson of repeated coordinates compresses
# about twelve to one, which is why this is affordable and 56 GB of scenes is
# not. These are the distilled record of what was looked at and what was
# found; everything upstream of them stays out.
!data/events/sar-*.geojson
!data/events/dark-*.geojson
!data/events/missed-*.geojson
!data/events/persistent-sites.geojson

# Logs
*.log
*.log.*

# Models
*.pkl
*.joblib

# Staging for deletion. The sandbox that writes into this repo cannot delete
# files, so anything on its way out is moved here instead. Never commit it.
_to_delete/
